Why don't people like the many-worlds interpretation of quantum mechanics?

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/ The many-worlds interpretation of quantum mechanics is not widely accepted due to several reasons. One of the main concerns is that it seems to contradict the principle of causality, which states that every event has a cause and effect relationship. In the many-worlds interpretation, every possible outcome of a quantum event exists in a separate universe, which means that there is no clear cause and effect relationship between events. This can be difficult to reconcile with our everyday experiences, where we expect events to have a clear cause and effect relationship. Additionally, the many-worlds interpretation can be mathematically complex and difficult to understand, which can make it challenging to communicate to the general public. Finally, some physicists argue that the many-worlds interpretation is not a testable hypothesis, as it is impossible to observe or measure the existence of other universes. Overall, while the many-worlds interpretation is an interesting and thought-provoking idea, it is not widely accepted due to its potential contradictions with our understanding of causality and its complexity.

